R"VG(
# letter_events_everyday.vg (H-04, the second pass): the letter's register for the everyday the history now keeps (its own stream: fairs, fires,
# storms, the fields' plagues, wonders, games, laws, trials, envoys, the works' disasters, prices, the herds, plays and songs, the ruler
# passing through): what a quiet year's letters are full of. `$e.what` is the kind's own word; `$e.value` a count; `$e.a` whoever it
# was about, when the history names one.

# ---- a fair ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

letter.fair
  (tense=past) We went to {let.fair.of} at {$e.place}. {let.fair.coda}
  (tense=past) {let.fair.of.cap} at {$e.place} drew {#$e.value:about}, and every one of them had an opinion about the {crop}. {let.fair.coda}
  (tense=past $e.here=1) {let.fair.of.cap} came to {$e.place} {deathwhen}, and the town is still sweeping up. {let.fair.coda}
let.fair.of
  ($e.what=beasts) the beast fair
  ($e.what=cloth) the cloth fair
  ($e.what=salt) the salt fair
  ($e.what=grain) the grain market
  ($e.what=pots) the potters' fair
  ($e.what=iron) the iron fair
  ($e.what=wine) the wine fair
  ($e.what=fish) the fish market
  ($e.what=furs) the fur market
  ($e.what=gathering) the great gathering
  ($e.what=horses) the horse fair
  ($e.what=books) the book fair
  ($e.what=machines) the show of machines
  ($e.what=flowers) the flower market
let.fair.of.cap
  ($e.what=beasts) The beast fair
  ($e.what=cloth) The cloth fair
  ($e.what=salt) The salt fair
  ($e.what=grain) The grain market
  ($e.what=pots) The potters' fair
  ($e.what=iron) The iron fair
  ($e.what=wine) The wine fair
  ($e.what=fish) The fish market
  ($e.what=furs) The fur market
  ($e.what=gathering) The great gathering
  ($e.what=horses) The horse fair
  ($e.what=books) The book fair
  ($e.what=machines) The show of machines
  ($e.what=flowers) The flower market
let.fair.coda (share=0.8)
  I bought {vessel.one} I did not need and sold nothing.
  The {herd} fetched a fair price, for once.
  There was a man with a {beast} that could count, or could look as if it could.
  I lost my purse by the {craft} stalls and found a cousin.
  The {craft} people from {city} were there, looking down on everything.
  We came home with {food} and a headache each.
  (age>=medieval) Everything costs {n} {coin} more than last year, and is worse.
  The {let.instrument} players were the best part, and free.

# ---- a fire --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

letter.fire
  (tense=past $e.what=street) A fire in {$e.place} took a whole street, {#$e.value|house} of it, before they got it out. {let.fire.coda}
  (tense=past $e.what=half) Half of {$e.place} burned {deathwhen}, {#$e.value:about|house}. {let.fire.coda}
  (tense=past $e.what=granary) The granary at {$e.place} burned, and the {crop} with it. {let.fire.coda}
  (tense=past $e.what=market) The market at {$e.place} burned down in the night; the {craft} stalls went first. {let.fire.coda}
  (tense=past $e.what=temple) Fire at {holyplace} in {$e.place}; the roof is gone and the walls are black. {let.fire.coda}
  (tense=past $e.what=mill) The mill at {$e.place} burned; flour burns like oil, they say, and it did. {let.fire.coda}
  (tense=past $e.what=library) The library at {$e.place} burned, with everything in it nobody had read. {let.fire.coda}
  (tense=past $e.what=harbour) Fire on the quays at {$e.place}; three boats went with it. {let.fire.coda}
  (tense=past $e.what=palace) Part of the palace at {$e.place} burned, and nobody will say how. {let.fire.coda}
  (tense=past $e.what=works) The works at {$e.place} burned; the sky was red all night. {let.fire.coda}
  (tense=past $e.what=grass) The grass fire came down off the hills to the edge of {$e.place}. {let.fire.coda}
  (tense=past $e.what=forest) The woods above {$e.place} burned for days, and we breathed it here. {let.fire.coda}
  (tense=past $e.what=tower) A tower at {$e.place} burned like a torch; you could see it from {mountain}. {let.fire.coda}
  (tense=past $e.what=camp) The fire at {$e.place} took the camp. {let.fire.coda}
let.fire.coda (share=0.75)
  Nobody we know was hurt.
  The {herd} were got out, mostly.
  We sent what we could: {vessel} and {food}.
  (contact=1) Half the town says it was the {other}, and the other half says it was the baker.
  We stood on the roof and watched, and then we went and carried water in {vessel}.
  The smell is still in my {garment.n}.
  {exclaim}, the whole street said, and then nobody said anything.
  They are already arguing about who builds what where, and in {material} or not.

# ---- a storm -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

letter.storm
  (tense=past $e.what=wind) A gale took the roofs off half of {$e.place}. {let.storm.coda}
  (tense=past $e.what=hail) Hail at {$e.place} as big as {fruit}, and the {crop} flattened. {let.storm.coda}
  (tense=past $e.what=snow) Snow at {$e.place} to the eaves; the {herd} were dug out alive, most of them. {let.storm.coda}
  (tense=past $e.what=dust) A dust storm over {$e.place} for two days; we breathed it and ate it and slept in it. {let.storm.coda}
  (tense=past $e.what=sea) A storm off the coast drove the boats onto the rocks at {$e.place}. {let.storm.coda}
  (tense=past $e.what=lightning) Lightning struck at {$e.place} and set {house.one} alight. {let.storm.coda}
  (tense=past $e.what=rain) It rained at {$e.place} as if the sky had been saving it for years. {let.storm.coda}
let.storm.coda (share=0.8)
  ($e.value>0) {#$e.value} dead, and the rest counting what is left.
  ($e.value=0) Nobody died, which everyone says lightly over their {drink} and nobody means lightly.
  We were lucky. The {roofs} held.
  The {bird} were all over the fields the next morning, looking for what the storm left.
  The old ones say they saw worse when they were young, when the {herd} were bigger.
  We tied the {vessel} down and sat in the dark and listened.

# ---- a plague on the fields or the herds ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

letter.pest
  (tense=past $e.what=locusts) The locusts came to {$e.place}; the sky went brown, and then the fields. {let.pest.coda}
  (tense=past $e.what=blight) The blight is in the {crop} at {$e.place}, black at the root. {let.pest.coda}
  (tense=past $e.what=murrain) The murrain has the {herd} at {$e.place}; they are burning them in pits. {let.pest.coda}
  (tense=past $e.what=rats) Rats in the stores at {$e.place}, more than anyone has seen. {let.pest.coda}
  (tense=past $e.what=worm) The worm is in the {fruit} at {$e.place}. {let.pest.coda}
  (tense=past $e.what=mildew) Mildew on the {crop2} at {$e.place}, grey as ash. {let.pest.coda}
  (tense=past $e.what=wolves) Wolves came down to {$e.place} in the cold and took {herd} from the pens. {let.pest.coda}
  (tense=past $e.what=mites) Mites in the {crop} at {$e.place}; the growers are beside themselves. {let.pest.coda}
let.pest.coda (share=0.8)
  It is not here yet; we look at our own {crop} every morning.
  We have done what the old ones say: {rite.do}. It has not helped them.
  The price of {crop} will go up; it always does when it is somebody else's field.
  {god} spare the {crop}, and us with it.
  The {bird} have never been fatter.

# ---- a wonder ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

letter.wonder
  (tense=past $e.what=calf) At {$e.place} a calf was born with two heads; the priests are arguing over which to listen to. {let.wonder.coda}
  (tense=past $e.what=fish) A fish as long as a boat was taken at {$e.place}. {let.wonder.coda}
  (tense=past $e.what=hoard) A man digging at {$e.place} found {vessel.one} full of old money. {let.wonder.coda}
  (tense=past $e.what=stone) A stone fell out of a clear sky at {$e.place}; it was warm for a day, they say. {let.wonder.coda}
  (tense=past $e.what=rain) It rained red at {$e.place}. {let.wonder.coda}
  (tense=past $e.what=spring) The spring at {$e.place} runs warm since the new moon. {let.wonder.coda}
  (tense=past $e.what=beast) A white {game.one} was seen above {$e.place}, and nobody would shoot at it. {let.wonder.coda}
  (tense=past $e.what=bones) Bones as big as a house were dug up at {$e.place}. {let.wonder.coda}
  (tense=past $e.what=twins) Three children at one birth at {$e.place}, all living. {let.wonder.coda}
  (tense=past $e.what=tree) The old {tree.n} at {$e.place} flowered out of all season. {let.wonder.coda}
  (tense=past $e.what=bell) The bell at {$e.place} rang by itself in the night. {let.wonder.coda}
  (tense=past $e.what=ruin) At {$e.place} they dug up walls older than anyone's, built by some people nobody remembers. {let.wonder.coda}
  (tense=past $e.what=signal) At {$e.place} they heard something out of the sky that was not noise. {let.wonder.coda}
let.wonder.coda (share=0.8)
  Everyone has gone to look but me and the {beast}.
  The priests at {holyplace} say it means something; the priests always say that.
  I will believe it when I see it, and then I will believe it less, like the {crop} prices.
  The children talk of nothing else, and play at it with the {beast}.
  It is {luck.good}, the old ones say, or {luck.bad}; they have not settled which.
  (age>=industrial) It was in the paper between the {crop} prices and the dead, so it must be true, or at least printed.

)VG"
